exports[`scopes create website projectable fields and examples > create website 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove node create website [--root-canvas | --space <id> | <other-target-space-options>] --url <url> [options]

Create a website node

Without explicit x/y, root-canvas placement searches next to you when run inside OpenCove and avoids Spaces. If your node belongs to another project, the search starts at the canvas origin. Use --focus to reveal the node. A --frame with both x and y places the node exactly there, even if it overlaps a Space.

Default target inside OpenCove: your current Space, or the root canvas when you are outside any Space. An explicit --project selects project scope for lists; creation also needs --root-canvas or a Space target.

Target container (choose one):
  --root-canvas                                 Create on the project's root canvas, outside every Space
  --space <id>                                  Select a space by its ID; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --space-name <name> [--project <id>]          Find a named space, optionally within a project
  --worker <id-or-name> --branch <branch>       Find a space by worker and Git branch
  --worker <id-or-name> --path <absolute-path>  Find a space by worker and working directory
  --project <id>                                Select the root-canvas project, or narrow a Space lookup; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Options:
  --url <url>                                Set the website address (required)
  --pinned                                   Keep the website pinned
  --session-mode <shared|incognito|profile>  Choose the browser session mode
  --profile <id>                             Select a browser or terminal profile
  --title <text>                             Set the node title
  --frame <json>                             Set x, y, width and height as a JSON object; appliedFrame and frameAdjusted report any changes
  --focus                                    Focus after creation
  -q, --quiet                                Print only the created object identifier followed by a newline

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: id, kind, title, projectId, spaceId, frame, frame.x, frame.y, frame.width, frame.height, status, sessionId, spawnedBy, lineageDepth, lineageStatus, data, data.kind, data.url, data.pinned, data.sessionMode, data.profileId

Field descriptions:
  spawnedBy      ID of the node that created this node; null for a root or unavailable lineage
  lineageDepth   A node started by the user has lineageDepth 1; each agent-created generation adds one. Null when lineage is unavailable
  lineageStatus  recorded when creation is recorded, root when no parent is recorded, unavailable when lineage cannot be read

Projection example:
  opencove node create website --url '<url>' --space space-123 --fields id,title
  {"revision":1,"projectId":"p","spaceId":"space-123","node":{"id":"n","title":"Website"}}

Examples:
  opencove node create website --space space-123 --url https://example.com
  opencove node create website --root-canvas --project project-123 --url https://example.com --title "Release review" --pretty

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`scopes update note projectable fields and examples > update note 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove node update note --node <id> [options]

Update a note node

Options:
  --text <text>         Set the note contents
  --text-file <path|->  Read text from a UTF-8 file, or use - for stdin
  --node <id>           Select a canvas node by its ID; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters) (required)
  --title <text>        Set the node title
  --frame <json>        Set x, y, width and height as a JSON object; appliedFrame and frameAdjusted report any changes
  --space <selector>    Require every node selector, including full IDs, to belong to this space; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --project <id>        Narrow target lookup to a project; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: id, kind, title, projectId, spaceId, frame, frame.x, frame.y, frame.width, frame.height, status, sessionId, data, data.kind, data.text

Projection example:
  opencove node update note --node '<id>' --fields id,title
  {"revision":1,"node":{"id":"n","title":"Note"}}

Examples:
  opencove node update note --node node-123 --text "Updated note"
  opencove node update note --node node-123 --title "Release review" --pretty

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

